<?xml version="1.0"?>
<!--
  OBD Platform - Phase C: THE directory. Replaces the image's stock default.xml.

  TEMPLATE - rendered at container start by infra/freeswitch/scripts/entrypoint.sh,
  which substitutes the two SIP password placeholders from the environment. No
  SIP credential is ever committed.

  This file exists as a single, owned directory because three separate defects
  were found by observation, all of them caused by the stock directory or by
  adding a second directory file beside it. See 13-TROUBLESHOOTING.md.

  1. THE STOCK USERS MUST GO.
     The stock file defines users 1000-1014 whose password is the publicly known
     value in vars.xml. Phase C had to enable auth-subscriptions to observe
     REGISTER / 401 / digest / 200 OK, so leaving them in place would have
     accepted a REGISTER for a stock user with a known password. Registration
     being enabled is only safe because this file removed them. Verified:
     a REGISTER for user 1000 with the stock password is answered 403.

  2. EXTENSION NUMBERS COLLIDE WITH THE STOCK DIRECTORY.
     The stock file also defines a user called 1001, in the same domain. With
     both files present the directory lookup finds the STOCK 1001, so a
     REGISTER for the test extension 1001 carrying the CORRECT password was
     answered:

         SIP/2.0 403 Forbidden

     and the test endpoint's gateway logged:

         sofia_reg.c:2661 platform-switch Failed Registration with status
         Forbidden [403]. failure #1

     Diagnostic worth keeping: a 403 on the authenticated retry does not mean
     "wrong password". Here it did not even mean "wrong user" - it meant the
     right user id was defined twice and the wrong definition won. Extension
     numbers are a shared namespace with whatever the image ships.

  3. THE dial-string PARAM IS NOT OPTIONAL.
     An earlier version of this file defined the two users but omitted the
     domain's dial-string parameter. Registration then worked, the endpoint
     registered, and a call to user/1002 produced a channel and an answered
     peer - but NO media, because the bridge never happened. FreeSWITCH said why,
     in the log, for the channel that tried to bridge:

         [ERR] mod_dptools.c:4419 No dial-string available, please check your
         user directory.

     user/<ext> is resolved through the directory's dial-string parameter. With
     it missing, the bridge target cannot be built, so the two legs are never
     connected: signalling looks completely healthy, and audio silently does
     not flow. That is the single most misleading failure mode in this whole
     phase, and it is invisible unless media is actually checked.

  WHY ONE FILE, NOT TWO
  Two files each declaring a domain with the same name is legal, and the
  directory then searches all of them - which is exactly what made defect 2 so
  hard to see. One file, one domain, two users: nothing to collide with.

  WHY THE USERS EXIST AT ALL
  Phase B deliberately disabled SIP registration, because the stock directory
  ships a publicly known default password and accepting a well-known credential
  is worse than accepting none. Phase C needs the registration flow observed, so
  registration is enabled - but only together with this file, which is the other
  half of that decision.

  SCOPE: these are DEVELOPMENT test identities. They are not production
  extensions, they have no DND or presence behaviour, and they must never be
  pointed at a real carrier.

  EDITING RULE (from Phase B, 13-TROUBLESHOOTING.md entry 1): a substitution
  token must never appear in a COMMENT - the renderer replaces every
  occurrence, so a token in prose would be replaced too and would write the
  live credential into the rendered file. The tokens below appear only in
  "password" and "vm-password" parameters, which is intentional.
-->
<domain name="$${domain}">

  <params>
    <!-- REQUIRED for user/<ext> to resolve. Without it a call to an extension
         produces a channel and an answered peer but never bridges, so there is
         no media and no DTMF. See the note at the top. -->
    <param name="dial-string" value="{$presence_id}${dialed_user}@{$dialed_domain}"/>
  </params>

  <groups>
    <group name="default">
      <users>
        <user id="1001">
          <param name="password" value="@@SIP_1001_PASSWORD@@"/>
          <param name="vm-password" value="@@SIP_1001_PASSWORD@@"/>
          <variables>
            <variable name="accountcode" value="1001"/>
            <variable name="user_context" value="default"/>
            <variable name="effective_caller_id_name" value="Ext 1001"/>
            <variable name="effective_caller_id_number" value="1001"/>
          </variables>
        </user>
        <user id="1002">
          <param name="password" value="@@SIP_1002_PASSWORD@@"/>
          <param name="vm-password" value="@@SIP_1002_PASSWORD@@"/>
          <variables>
            <variable name="accountcode" value="1002"/>
            <variable name="user_context" value="default"/>
            <variable name="effective_caller_id_name" value="Ext 1002"/>
            <variable name="effective_caller_id_number" value="1002"/>
          </variables>
        </user>
      </users>
    </group>
  </groups>

</domain>
